1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,910
Amber: Hello William, this is Amber.

2
00:00:06,910 --> 00:00:13,660
You said to phone if I wanted to get more information about the job agency you mentioned.

3
00:00:13,660 --> 00:00:15,680
Is now a good time?

4
00:00:15,680 --> 00:00:18,490
William: Hi Amber, yes, fine.

5
00:00:18,490 --> 00:00:23,570
So, the agency I was talking about is called Bankside, they're based in Docklands.

6
00:00:23,570 --> 00:00:27,560
I can tell you the address now, 497 Eastside.

7
00:00:27,560 --> 00:00:33,530
Amber: Ok, thanks. So, is there anyone in particular I should speak to there?

8
00:00:33,530 --> 00:00:37,460
William: The agent I always deal with is called Becky Jamieson.

9
00:00:37,460 --> 00:00:40,740
Amber: Let me write that down. Becky...

10
00:00:40,740 --> 00:00:48,620
William: Jamieson, J-A-M. I-E-S-O-N.

11
00:00:48,620 --> 00:00:51,420
Amber: Do you have her direct line?

12
00:00:51,420 --> 00:00:54,430
William: Yes, it's in my contact somewhere.

13
00:00:54,430 --> 00:01:01,720
Right, here we are, 07866 510333.

14
00:01:01,720 --> 00:01:04,800
I wouldn't call her until the afternoon if I were you.

15
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:09,210
She's always really busy in the morning trying to fill last minute vacancies.

16
00:01:09,210 --> 00:01:10,586
She's really helpful and friendly,

17
00:01:10,586 --> 00:01:14,990
so I'm sure it would be worth getting in touch with her for an informal chat.

18
00:01:14,990 --> 00:01:19,800
Amber: It's mainly clerical and admin jobs they do, where isn't it?

19
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:21,000
William: That's right.

20
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:24,690
I know you're hoping to find a full-time job in the media eventually.

21
00:01:24,690 --> 00:01:28,317
But Becky mostly recruits temporary staff for the finance sector,

22
00:01:28,317 --> 00:01:32,670
which will look good on your CV and generally pays better too.

23
00:01:32,670 --> 00:01:38,330
Amber: Yeah, I'm just a bit worried because I don't have much office experience.

24
00:01:38,330 --> 00:01:39,390
William: I wouldn't worry.

25
00:01:39,390 --> 00:01:43,110
They'll probably start you with a receptionist or something like that.

26
00:01:43,110 --> 00:01:46,264
So, what's important for that kind of job isn't

27
00:01:46,264 --> 00:01:50,470
so much having business skills or knowing lots of different computer systems.

28
00:01:50,470 --> 00:01:52,790
It's communication that really matters.

29
00:01:52,790 --> 00:01:57,240
So, you'd be fine there and you'll pick up office skills really quickly on the job.

30
00:01:57,240 --> 00:01:58,980
It's not that complicated.

31
00:01:58,980 --> 00:02:00,560
Amber: Okay good.

32
00:02:00,560 --> 00:02:05,240
So how long do people generally need temporary staff for?

33
00:02:05,240 --> 00:02:09,370
It would be great if I could get something lasting at least a month.

34
00:02:09,370 --> 00:02:11,084
William: That shouldn't be too difficult,

35
00:02:11,084 --> 00:02:15,940
but you're more likely to be offered something for a week at first, which might get extended.

36
00:02:15,940 --> 00:02:18,930
It's unusual to be sent somewhere for just a day or two.

37
00:02:18,930 --> 00:02:22,380
Amber: Right, I've heard the pay isn't too bad

38
00:02:22,940 --> 00:02:25,660
better than working in a shop or a restaurant.

39
00:02:25,660 --> 00:02:27,120
William: Oh yes, definitely.

40
00:02:27,120 --> 00:02:30,780
The hourly rate is about 10 pounds, 11 if you're lucky.

41
00:02:30,780 --> 00:02:32,630
Amber: That's pretty good.

42
00:02:32,630 --> 00:02:36,900
I was only expecting to get 8 or 9 pounds an hour.

43
00:02:36,900 --> 00:02:40,280
William: Do you want me to tell you anything about the registration process?

44
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:44,330
Amber: Yes, please. I know you have had an interview.

45
00:02:44,330 --> 00:02:46,360
William: The interview usually takes about an hour

46
00:02:46,440 --> 00:02:49,150
and you should arrange that about a week in advance.

47
00:02:49,150 --> 00:02:53,300
Amber: I suppose I should dress smartly if it's for office work.

48
00:02:53,300 --> 00:02:56,460
I can probably borrow a suit from mum.

49
00:02:56,460 --> 00:03:00,730
William: Good idea, it's better to look too smart than too casual.

50
00:03:00,730 --> 00:03:05,940
Amber: Will I need to bring copies of my exam certificate or anything like that.

51
00:03:05,940 --> 00:03:08,430
William: No, they don't need to see those, I don't think.

52
00:03:08,430 --> 00:03:10,650
Amber: What about my passport?

53
00:03:10,650 --> 00:03:12,860
William: Oh yes, they will ask to see that.

54
00:03:12,860 --> 00:03:13,990
Amber: Ok.

55
00:03:13,990 --> 00:03:16,480
William: I wouldn't get stressed about the interview, though.

56
00:03:16,480 --> 00:03:19,420
It's just a chance for them to build relationship with you.

57
00:03:19,420 --> 00:03:22,280
So, they can try and match you to a job which you like.

58
00:03:22,280 --> 00:03:28,480
So, there are questions about personality that they always ask candidates, fairly basic ones.

59
00:03:28,480 --> 00:03:33,570
And they probably won't ask anything too difficult like what your plans are for the future.

60
00:03:33,570 --> 00:03:35,850
Amber: Oh, hope not.

61
00:03:35,850 --> 00:03:39,670
William: Anyway, there are lots of benefits to using an agency.

62
00:03:39,670 --> 00:03:42,020
For example, the interview will be useful

63
00:03:42,020 --> 00:03:44,577
because they'll give you feedback on your performance,

64
00:03:44,577 --> 00:03:46,540
so you can improve next time.

65
00:03:46,540 --> 00:03:50,480
Amber: And they'll have access to jobs which aren't advertised.

66
00:03:50,480 --> 00:03:54,360
William: Exactly, most temporary jobs aren't advertised.

67
00:03:54,360 --> 00:03:59,780
Amber: And I expect finding a temporary job this way takes a lot less time.

68
00:03:59,780 --> 00:04:03,730
It's much easier than bringing up individual company.

69
00:04:03,730 --> 00:04:08,730
William: Yes, indeed. Well, I think I've covered everything... (fade out)
